(a) At $t=0$:
$\begin{aligned}200&=300+A\\A&=-100\end{aligned}$
At $t=4$:
$\begin{aligned}100&=300-100e^{4k}\\-200&=-100e^{4k}\\e^{4k}&=2\\k&=\frac14\ln2\end{aligned}$
Thus $Q=300-100e^{(\ln2)t/4}$.
$\begin{aligned}\text{(b)}\quad&0&=300-100e^{kt}\\&e^{kt}&=3\\&t&=\frac{\ln3}{k}\\&&=\frac{4\ln3}{\ln2}\\&&\approx6.3\end{aligned}$
(c) The graph starts at $(0,200)$, decreases with increasing steepness, and crosses the time axis at $t=\frac{4\ln3}{\ln2}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=0.12mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(8.3,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,-125)--(0,340);
\path (8.3,0) node[right] {$t$};
\path (0,340) node[above] {$Q$};
\draw[black,dashed] (0,300)--(7.8,300);
\path (5,300) node[above] {$Q=300$};
\draw[black] (-0.12,200)--(0.12,200);
\path (0,200) node[left] {$200$};
\draw[black,thick,->,domain=0:8,samples=80] plot (\x,{300-100*exp(ln(2)*\x/4)});
\fill[black] (0,200) circle (1.2pt);
\draw[black] (6.33985,-8)--(6.33985,8);
\path (6.33985,0) node[above right] {$\frac{4\ln3}{\ln2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Since $k>0$ and $A=-100\ne0$, $Q\to-\infty$ as $t\to\infty$. There is no finite limiting value; the fixed offset $300$ is not a future limit. If $Q$ represents a non-negative quantity, the physical model ends when $Q=0$.
